  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1217 AM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nA few showers have been slow to disintegrate over the evening, \nand there are a few pockets of fog around the Iron Range where \nrain has fallen during the afternoon. Otherwise, there are \nplenty of clear skies and quiet conditions out there, which will\npersist through the early morning.\n\nToday, surface high pressure moves in from the west, and we will \nhave some weak shortwaves passing by aloft that should keep plenty \nof clouds in place. The air should be pretty dry, so rain isn't \nexpected, but if there were a few spotty sprinkles in the Arrowhead \nduring the afternoon, it wouldn't be shocking. Light to calm winds \ntonight will lead to partially ideal radiational cooling conditions, \nthough clouds will stick around. Areas of frost will be likely.\n\nThe surface high will move east tonight and we'll get \nsoutherly/southeasterly return flow developing on Friday. \nTemperatures will start to warm up as a result. Other than that, \npretty quiet weather aside from some clouds associated with warm air \nadvection. \n\nA broad upper-level ridge axis will settle over the Northland \nSaturday night with a low-level jet developing with strong warm air \nadvection. The air is expected to be pretty dry, and skies will \nlikely start to clear out from south to north. Winds are expected to \nbe breezy all night, steadily increasing in speed.\n\nOn Saturday, a low pressure system to the west with the upper-level \nridge moving east just slightly, but still pretty much overhead, \nwill lead to a very tight pressure gradient with strong warm and dry \nair advection through the day. Put simply, Saturday is looking hot, \ndry, and windy with the majority of GEFS members in the 90th \npercentile or higher of climatology for the HDW Index. Highs will \nlikely reach into the 80s for many places and wind gusts in the 30-\n40 mph range will be common, perhaps with some gusts approaching 45 \nmph, especially along the South Shore. Near-critical fire weather \nconditions are likely, and if conditions trend drier, critical fire \nweather conditions can't be ruled out.\n\nWe'll keep within the warm sector Saturday night, and low \ntemperatures in the 50s and 60s will be right about where normal \nhighs are this time of year. On Sunday, we'll get a cold front \nmoving in from northwest to southeast. We could get some rain in \nnorth-central Minnesota as early as Sunday late afternoon/evening \nwith the cold front. A secondary trough aloft may bring a more \nwidespread area of rain on Monday. Much cooler temperatures will \nfollow with northwest flow Monday night. Expect some temperatures to \nfall below freezing up north.\n\nModels differ on how late rain may exit Monday night, and it's \npossible there could be lingering light showers of rain or mixed \nprecipitation into Tuesday, but most likely we will dry out with \nhigh pressure moving in. Temperatures will be seasonably cool, and \nexpect more below-freezing temperatures Tuesday night.",
